#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4ae9d6b-0976-57c8-b4d6-8fa0f04b9518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p-5}{p}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242866} {:answer/id #uuid "18fdd50c-5776-5421-9044-525658077004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2p+5}{p}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242867} {:answer/id #uuid "a8aa330a-39a1-5533-87bd-9f12470df209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(p+5)}{p}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242868} {:answer/id #uuid "d88ff7dd-55d4-51b2-8a10-8d04a1ebd62d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2p-5}{p}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242869} {:answer/id #uuid "bf67acc6-8a4a-5066-b5c5-7fbcece54fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p+5}{p}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18fdd50c-5776-5421-9044-525658077004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2p+5}{p}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242867} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba74deb7-0fac-5786-81f4-14bcd6ac98b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "169f878e-5da3-5a3e-8d61-1e5d55f0d0c7" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-113217" :question/problem "Write $3+\\frac{5-p}{p}$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{5-p}{p}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $p$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{p}{p}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{3p}{p}+\\frac{5-p}{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3p}{p}+\\frac{5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{3p+5-p}{p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2p+5}{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df136a3c-0c94-558d-8875-21bb302f26a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{w+8}{w+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242873} {:answer/id #uuid "047cf466-15a1-57d1-86a4-70733122e133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3(w-4)}{w+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242874} {:answer/id #uuid "94e3ab92-1f0e-57a3-a793-d52dafe8e1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{w+24}{w+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242875} {:answer/id #uuid "991076ce-2af0-55f2-bfe6-dc38edd9ed6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(w+8)}{w+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242876} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab30660-4d02-5fe7-90e2-d43f7ea1ca67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3w+10}{w+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "991076ce-2af0-55f2-bfe6-dc38edd9ed6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3(w+8)}{w+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242876} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e452f2a-dcba-5730-9761-703baf6d43d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8208ac8f-2edb-5e55-90bc-68d51c8ee105" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-113225" :question/problem "Write $\\frac{3w}{w+4}-6$ as a single fraction." :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $6=\\frac{6}{1}$, we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $w+4$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the second fraction by $w+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{w+4}{w+4}\\cdot \\frac{6}{1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3w}{w+4}-\\frac{6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6(w+4)}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3w-6w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{-3w-24}{w+4} & = \\\\ -\\frac{3(w+8)}{w+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "63080bb8-7a6a-505d-813a-e6c99d337064" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting a Number and a Rational Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62ca910b-92eb-5007-8b17-2edecc397e25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2q+1}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242880} {:answer/id #uuid "42e4f578-63e2-5656-ad21-30d93cd94315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4q+1}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242881} {:answer/id #uuid "2d67a5b7-dcb1-5c9a-a5de-f484eedc1a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(2q+1)}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242882} {:answer/id #uuid "868e8460-7487-55cd-985e-e67611b4c95b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242883} {:answer/id #uuid "4f349683-2388-510b-887d-5c0fdf890519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6+q}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d67a5b7-dcb1-5c9a-a5de-f484eedc1a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(2q+1)}{q}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242882} :answer-field/id #uuid "27c24175-fb65-5a05-88b8-f420e840cfa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ff0d467-caec-5c0d-8ad6-c8bce8cc4b0b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-3742" :question/problem "$4-\\frac{(-2)}{q}=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that $4=\\frac{4}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q}=.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $q$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $q:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{q}{q}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q}{q}-\\frac{(-2)}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4q}{q}-\\frac{(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q-(-2)}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{4q+2}{q} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(2q+1)}{q}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3db20b82-9561-5c54-8e12-8914bb6747c3" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7929bde1-56f6-50e6-922e-ff98ca2945bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6+x}{3x(1+z)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242887} {:answer/id #uuid "b50a07a4-88b1-555a-82dc-c234238ef7df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{6(1+z)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242888} {:answer/id #uuid "53ebc276-de74-53da-81c4-fb98ee52ac80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{x(1+z)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242889} {:answer/id #uuid "b2862fef-0a01-5f96-b4b6-334165c988d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3-x}{3x(1+z)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242890} {:answer/id #uuid "d8838710-15a2-5932-af10-43fca6b64619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6x(1+z)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7929bde1-56f6-50e6-922e-ff98ca2945bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6+x}{3x(1+z)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242887} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6f75636-7cd2-58c5-82d9-faeeeb7512cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a5b4771-1158-5398-b8a1-3658dbca7492" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-69903" :question/problem "$\\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{1}{3(1+z)}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $3x(1+z),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit.\n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $3$, and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{1}{3(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x(1+z)}+\\frac{x}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{3x(1+z)}+\\frac{x}{3x(1+z)} & = \\\\ \\frac{6+x}{3x(1+z)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3db20b82-9561-5c54-8e12-8914bb6747c3" :knowledge-point/title "Adding and Subtracting Rational Expressions With No Common Factors in the Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2c9c902-abee-5a3a-89a0-57cdca476b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2wz-4z+3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242894} {:answer/id #uuid "78892bff-703c-568b-965b-b2a5de7c1864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2wz-3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242895} {:answer/id #uuid "29e9c33e-e143-5bc0-af93-91981c446fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2wz-3z}{(z-1)(2w-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242896} {:answer/id #uuid "a008b897-1f0d-5efc-8252-d783b1545b8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4w-2wz}{(z-1)(2w-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242897} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1f829e-5b7d-5bd2-90fd-a52349346cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2wz-4w-3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78892bff-703c-568b-965b-b2a5de7c1864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2wz-3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242895} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2c58060-ddbd-5a62-a914-e817afee1448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c462f9dc-0271-5628-94dd-832997d4ff88" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-70295" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{z-1}+\\frac{2w}{2w-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we need to put the fractions over a common denominator of $(z-1)(2w-3),$ since there are no common factors in either denominator that we can exploit. \n\nTo do this, we multiply the top and bottom of the first fraction by $2w-3,$ and the top and bottom of the second fraction by $z-1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{z-1}+\\frac{2w}{2w-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-3}{2w-3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{z-1}+\\frac{z-1}{z-1}\\cdot \\frac{2w}{2w-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}+\\frac{(z-1)2w}{(z-1)(2w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(2w-3)+(z-1)2w}{(z-1)(2w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2w-3+2wz-2w}{(z-1)(2w-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2wz-3}{(z-1)(2w-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
